<title>NEW ZEALAND: Air NZ sees no disruption from Boeing rudder refit.</title>
<headline>Air NZ sees no disruption from Boeing rudder refit.</headline>
<dateline>WELLINGTON 1997-01-16</dateline>
<text>
<p>Air New Zealand Ltd said on Thursday it expected no disruption or cost impact from having to modify the rudder systems of its 11 Boeing 737 jets.</p>
<p>U.S. Vice President Al Gore said earlier that federal safety officials would order Boeing Co to modify the rudder systems of the 737s, two of which have been involved in still unexplained fatal crashes.</p>
<p>The changes are aimed at preventing inadvertent rudder movement which could throw a plane into a sudden, uncontrolled roll.  </p>
<p>Air NZ spokesman David Beatson said the required work would be carried out over a three-year period and the cost would be borne by Boeing. &quot;We don't expect it to have any significant effect on us,&quot; he said.</p>
<p>-- Wellington newsroom 64 4 473-4746</p>
